#let abstract-en = [
Equitable access to financial services in Nicaragua's agricultural sector represents a critical determinant of socioeconomic development; nonetheless, empirical evaluations regarding its structural impact across rural holdings remain scarce. This research analyzes the effect of financing on rural labor absorption and productive diversification using microdata from the IV National Agricultural Census (CENAGRO 2011). As an integrative data science capstone project, it combines dimensional OLAP modeling and vectorized data transformation pipelines. Methodologically, a non-experimental cross-sectional design was conducted, analyzing over 262,000 agricultural holdings through a modern analytical architecture powered by DuckDB and Evidence.dev. The empirical findings reveal a severe entry barrier where merely 15.9% of agricultural producers managed to apply for credit, despite a commercial banking approval rate of 92.6%. Among capitalized holdings, median labor intensity increased by 171.6% in medium and large units, albeit heavily concentrated in seasonal labor (68.6% of holdings operate exclusively with temporary labor). Furthermore, credit access reduced the pasture-to-crop ratio from 4.18 to 2.71, raising permanent crop land allocation from 13.6% to 22.9% and shifting modal crop complexity toward three cultivars. In conclusion, formal credit dynamizes immediate operational capacity and productive intensification, but reinforces precarious transitory contracts, emphasizing the need for targeted rural credit programs combined with structural technical assistance.
]

